{"name":"Drake Tooth","hex":"#bbb0a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"drake","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A male duck (bird)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dragon.","A lesser draconic creature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/drake"},{"term":"tooth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard, calcareous structure present in the mouth of many vertebrate animals, generally used for biting and chewing food.","A sharp projection on the blade of a saw or similar implement."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide or furnish with teeth.","To indent; to jag."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tooth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tooth","blocks":[{"text":"A tooth is a hard, calcified structure found in the jaws of many vertebrates and used to break down food. Some animals, particularly carnivores and omnivores, also use teeth to help with capturing or wounding prey, tearing food, for defensive purposes, to intimidate other animals often including their own, or to carry prey or their young. The roots of teeth are covered by gums. Teeth are not made of bone, but rather of multiple tissues of varying density and hardness that originate from the outermost embryonic germ layer, the ectoderm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png/330px-Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooth"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg/500px-Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg/1280px-Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mammoth_ivory_